## Weight management programmes

These help you make lasting changes to what you eat and how active you are, with support along the way.

- **Local weight management services:** your GP can refer you. Some councils also offer free programmes you can join yourself.
- **NHS Digital Weight Management Programme (England):** a free 12 week online programme for adults living with obesity who also have diabetes or high blood pressure. Your GP or pharmacist can refer you.
- **Specialist weight management services:** for people with severe obesity or complex needs. Teams may include doctors, dietitians and psychologists.

## Medicines

Medicines can help alongside changes to eating and activity. They are not a quick fix, and weight often returns if they are stopped.

- **Orlistat** stops some of the fat in food being absorbed.
- **Semaglutide (Wegovy)** and **tirzepatide (Mounjaro)** are weekly injections that make you feel full sooner. NICE has approved them for some adults, based on things like BMI and related health conditions. They are prescribed through specialist services and, increasingly, GP practices. Access is being rolled out in stages.

Common side effects include feeling sick, diarrhoea and constipation. These medicines are not suitable during pregnancy or when trying for a baby.

{% callout type="warn" title="Stay safe" %}
Only get weight loss medicines on prescription from the NHS or a **registered UK pharmacy** after a proper health check. Never buy them from social media, beauty salons or unregulated websites. Fake products can be dangerous.
{% /callout %}

## Weight loss surgery

Surgery, such as a **gastric band** or **gastric bypass**, may be offered if:

- your BMI is **40 or more**, or **35 or more** with a condition that could improve with weight loss, such as type 2 diabetes or high blood pressure
- you have tried other ways of losing weight
- you are fit for surgery and willing to follow up with long-term care

The BMI levels are lower for some ethnic groups and for people recently diagnosed with type 2 diabetes. Surgery is a big step, and you will need lifelong check-ups and vitamin supplements.

## Support for your mental health

Many people living with obesity also experience low mood, anxiety or disordered eating, and have faced stigma. Support for your mental health is part of good care. Ask your GP, or in England refer yourself to **NHS Talking Therapies**.

